WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
These are images you've probably never seen&nbsp;
before; it's an atomic bomb detonating in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.540
the heart of the Australian outback. We're used&nbsp;
to hearing about nuclear testing in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.400
States, but many Aussies don't realise that&nbsp;
our own country was also a testing ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:21.060
The price Australia paid for this was steep with&nbsp;
devastating impacts on our people and environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.760
And to make matters worse, Australia never&nbsp;
got to share in any benefits from these tests.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:32.040
It was the British using Australia to test its&nbsp;
bombs. They claimed to have cleaned up the site&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.980
before handing it back to Australia with a&nbsp;
report saying it was now safe. But in 1984,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:43.380
more than 20 years after the test program&nbsp;
ended, Australian scientists walked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.180
land with Geiger counters to check for&nbsp;
radiation and got the shock of their lives.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.060
Expecting to find low levels of contamination,&nbsp;
they wore shorts and no protective clothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.060 --> 00:01:01.260
but instead, their readings fluctuated wildly. As&nbsp;
they stumbled over rocks and soil, they realised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:07.320
they were treading on plutonium-soaked earth. They&nbsp;
even found chunks of the deadly substance itself.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:13.380
Plutonium, a man-made element, is created&nbsp;
in a nuclear reactor by bombarding uranium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:19.920
with neutrons. The isotope in question here is&nbsp;
plutonium-239, which has a half-life of over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.920
24,000 years so remains dangerous&nbsp;
practically forever. Inhaling just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.280
20 milligrams of plutonium&nbsp;
can cause a fatal illness.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.360
The scientists were there to survey&nbsp;
the land before it was to be handed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.580
back to the traditional owners, the Maralinga&nbsp;
Tjarutja people, who were displaced in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.620
hundreds by the tests. The Australians were&nbsp;
shocked and unprepared for what they found.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:49.320
The British had claimed to have cleaned up&nbsp;
any contamination, and a report put together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:56.700
by UK scientist Noah Pearce made no mention of&nbsp;
these deadly plutonium fragments. It's enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:02.460
radioactive material that international laws&nbsp;
would require it to be safeguarded from bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:08.280
actors trying to make a bomb. It also contains&nbsp;
secret traces of how the British bombs were put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:14.760
together but was just left lying out in the&nbsp;
desert unguarded and undisclosed for decades.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:21.300
In the 50s and 60s the British detonated 12&nbsp;
major atomic bombs and performed hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.920
of related experiments across Australia,&nbsp;
leaving parts of the country permanently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.080
contaminated by deadly radiation.&nbsp;
It’s a story of negligence and harm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:36.840
that starts with a few physicists and a&nbsp;
seemingly worthless heavy metal: uranium.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:40.260
It's the heaviest of all natural elements and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.560
known to have more neutrons&nbsp;
than protons in its nucleus.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.300
Bombarding the nucleus with neutrons&nbsp;
at high speed could cause it to split,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:54.300
undergoing nuclear fission. In 1938,&nbsp;
researchers in Paris discovered that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:59.880
the uranium atom flings out extra neutrons&nbsp;
when split. Noone initially thought those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.860
neutrons could be harnessed or controlled&nbsp;
for anything, but soon theories of a chain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:10.500
reaction made a bomb possible. Neils Bohr and&nbsp;
John Wheeler published a memo on the fission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:16.020
process just three days before war broke out,&nbsp;
putting everyone on the same starting line.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.860
Uranium-235 could easily sustain a chain&nbsp;
reaction where the neutrons flung out by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:29.280
one nucleus splitting would cause other nuclei&nbsp;
to split, each time releasing a great deal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.740
energy. A material reaches critical mass&nbsp;
when more neutrons can go off and split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:40.980
other nuclei than are lost in the reaction. Otto&nbsp;
Frisch and Rudolf Peierls working in England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:47.160
found that just a few pounds of uranium-235&nbsp;
were needed to reach critical mass.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.020
The British secretly worked on this idea&nbsp;
under a deliberately boring-sounding name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.300
Tube Alloys. The British were well in&nbsp;
front of the Americans at this stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:03.300
and in 1941 British research was given&nbsp;
to the US, where it fueled the Manhattan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:09.840
project that culminated in the dropping of&nbsp;
bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in 1945.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:16.740
After this, the US didn't need the British&nbsp;
anymore, and after a couple of high-profile atomic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:23.700
spies were discovered, they didn't trust the UK&nbsp;
to keep secrets. UK physicists were shut out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:31.800
and the Atomic Energy Act was introduced by the&nbsp;
US in 1946 that banned collaboration on nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:37.380
weapons development. It took the British by&nbsp;
surprise, and this betrayal was why Britain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:42.660
turned to developing their own bombs and&nbsp;
ended up coming to Australia to test them.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.720
Australia had its own&nbsp;
reasons for allowing them to.&nbsp;

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:51.060
1. Australia wanted to maximise the value&nbsp;
of their extensive uranium resources.&nbsp;

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:56.160
2. They thought that helping the British&nbsp;
could protect them in case of nuclear war.&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.580
3. They wanted to learn from the British in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.940
case they decided to make their&nbsp;
own nuclear weapons or energy.&nbsp;

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:09.780
However, the British provided no nuclear secrets&nbsp;
to Australia, and Australia was reluctant to ask.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:15.660
Australia was an ideal location for the&nbsp;
British tests. There were vast areas of land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:21.360
we had a prime minister who said yes without&nbsp;
asking too many questions and well it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:27.600
wasn't Britain and any harm would be out of&nbsp;
sight and out of mind for them. The British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:33.840
arrived in Australia in 1952 and tested their&nbsp;
first atomic bomb at the Montebello Islands.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.980
They were keen to test the effects&nbsp;
of detonating a weapon in the hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:42.900
of a ship and analyse the specifics&nbsp;
of a phenomenon called base surge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.620
which involved a large upward&nbsp;
movement of radioactive water.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:54.840
The weapon was 25 kilotons which is 10 kilotons&nbsp;
more than the bomb that was dropped on Hiroshima&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.820
so it was a very powerful weapon. It&nbsp;
was one of the most powerful, not the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:07.380
but one of the most powerful bombs detonated in&nbsp;
Australia. It did spread fallout over a wide area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:13.320
that is still actually being investigated&nbsp;
scientifically. I do know from my own research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:19.320
that the fallout went as far as Brisbane&nbsp;
so it was tracked across the continent.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:26.220
For the next tests, the British moved to Emu Field&nbsp;
in the Australian desert. There Operation Totem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:33.600
detonated more bombs, this time testing the impact&nbsp;
of differing proportions of plutonium. They left a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:38.580
kilometre-wide patch where the earth was heated&nbsp;
to such high temperatures that it became glass.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:45.240
Totem One which was detonated on the 15th&nbsp;
of October of 1953 created a very mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:51.900
phenomenon that later became known as the Black&nbsp;
Mist. We still don't fully understand what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:57.720
Black Mist was. We do know that it traveled&nbsp;
about 170 kilometers to the northeast of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:05.760
the Emu Field site where it rolled over largely&nbsp;
indigenous populations around the settlements of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:12.780
Wallatinna and Granite Downs, that kind of area.&nbsp;
We know that it was a very strange phenomenon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:22.020
a greasy black cloud that had a strange smell.&nbsp;
Some people said it smelled like kerosene other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:28.440
people said it smelled like a dead kangaroo and&nbsp;
it stuck to everything and very soon after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:34.560
Black Mist rolled over those communities people&nbsp;
started to get very ill and some people died.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:42.540
Some people had chronic illnesses, some people&nbsp;
reported eye problems and indeed blindness.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:48.720
Next Britain's Focus shifted to testing a&nbsp;
hydrogen bomb before an international treaty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:54.960
to limit nuclear weapons testing took effect.&nbsp;
Since hydrogen bombs are even more powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:01.320
than Atomic ones, Australia did actually put&nbsp;
its foot down and said no to testing them. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:07.320
instead the British just tested a device that&nbsp;
could operate as a fission trigger for one and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:13.140
this remains the biggest Atomic device ever&nbsp;
to explode in Australia. It was tested back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:21.660
at the Montebello Islands in June 1956. Its Cloud&nbsp;
rose 14 000 meters high, so different from what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:26.400
authorities had predicted that many people&nbsp;
thought something had gone terribly wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:34.020
Back in the desert in 1956-57 this time&nbsp;
at a more permanent base at Maralinga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:40.740
seven more atomic bombs were detonated as&nbsp;
part of Operations Buffalo and Antler. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:47.820
were detonated in a few ways; from steel towers,&nbsp;
dropped from aircraft or from tethered balloons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:54.480
300 meters high. There was also Buffalo 2&nbsp;
detonated at ground level that created a 21&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:01.020
meter deep bomb crater. Damage from these tests&nbsp;
spread over a large part of the continent but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:06.780
the worst was still to come. The most dangerous&nbsp;
scientific experiments in Australia's history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:15.480
were the Vixen B series of so-called minor&nbsp;
trials conducted between 1959 and 1963.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:22.080
The minor trials, and that name is rather&nbsp;
ironic and it's kind of deliberate as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:27.240
it was intended to be misleading, they&nbsp;
were extremely dangerous and extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:36.180
damaging. The Vixen B trial involved using&nbsp;
conventional explosives to detonate a lump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:41.760
of plutonium. This is a test to see what&nbsp;
would happen if an aircraft carrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:48.720
an atomic weapon crashed or caught fire or you&nbsp;
know there was some accident or even terrorism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:54.060
There were 12 Vixen B tests altogether and in&nbsp;
one of those we know for sure a chain reaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.060 --> 00:10:01.260
did eventuate, so that is it was a small&nbsp;
atomic weapon. The rest of them were more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:07.620
like what would be known today as dirty bombs,&nbsp;
suitcase bombs, they were they just spread the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:16.200
radioactive material in a plume shape across the&nbsp;
landscape. Essentially Vixen B left more than 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:23.460
kilograms of plutonium-239 at the site without&nbsp;
any safeguards and that had implications later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:28.560
for international agreements that Australia&nbsp;
was party to but also just for anyone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.980
happened to enter that territory they were at&nbsp;
risk of exposure to highly dangerous material.

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:41.400
Secrecy and coverups meant the British nuclear&nbsp;
tests in Australia remained mysterious for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:47.820
decades. Until a Royal Commission presented&nbsp;
the findings of its investigations 1985,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:51.660
Australia really had no idea what had gone on.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:55.560
The tests massively disrupted&nbsp;
and harmed the Aboriginal people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.560 --> 00:11:02.580
who didn't have the federal vote and were not&nbsp;
counted in the census until 1967. In Maralinga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:07.920
people spotted living on the land were&nbsp;
directed to a station at Yalata. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.580
small groups did walk across the&nbsp;
grounds after the tests began.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:19.800
The native Patrol Officer William McDougall and&nbsp;
his offside Bob McCauley they had the task of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:25.740
keeping Aboriginal people away from the site&nbsp;
and that was an impossible task given the size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:34.560
of the territory and the the routes between&nbsp;
water holes and people visiting family you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:40.020
know it was a it was a busy place and people&nbsp;
were traversing the territory all the time.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:47.280
One particularly famous example of that happening&nbsp;
during the tests was the Milpuddie family. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:54.840
walked onto the Maralinga range in 1957&nbsp;
several months after a test had been held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.840 --> 00:12:01.920
at a site called Marcoo, the only bomb test at&nbsp;
Maralinga that involved detonating a device on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.320
the ground and so it created a crater, a&nbsp;
very deep and wide crater. The Milppuddie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:13.680
family camped by the side of that crater. They&nbsp;
weren't found until a patrol the next morning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:22.140
and they were hastily taken away it was the&nbsp;
father mother and children and some dogs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:30.300
and they were rather brutally showered&nbsp;
and had geiger counters run over them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:36.300
On the orders of Minister for Supply Howard&nbsp;
Beale their dogs were shot in front of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:43.200
Edie Milpuddie, the wife was pregnant at the&nbsp;
time and she lost her baby soon after. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:48.660
were plenty of other instances where people were&nbsp;
treated just as harshly placed onto trucks taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:54.720
away to places on The Great Australian bite to&nbsp;
missions and places like that were you know losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.720 --> 00:13:02.520
contact with their land and so the Milpuddie&nbsp;
story is an important one on a human level to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:09.240
understand what happened to real people who were&nbsp;
displaced and mistreated by the British trials.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:12.120
The British did not accurately&nbsp;
describe what they were leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:17.160
behind. Low-rank military personnel put&nbsp;
together the data in the Pearce report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:23.460
and their measurements lacked an understanding of&nbsp;
how radiation could be dispersed. That was strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:29.640
since understanding the dispersal of radiation&nbsp;
and its consequences for the environment was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:34.320
an important part of what the British hoped&nbsp;
to gain from the tests in the first place.

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:08.040
There's still quite a bit of controversy&nbsp;
about whether or not that was adequate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:16.320
however I'm satisfied that the site is much&nbsp;
safer than it was before the cleanup. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:21.060
is still limits on visiting there. There are&nbsp;
time limits on how long you can spend on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:26.580
site and no one can stay there indefinitely in&nbsp;
the Forward Range part of the of the Maralinga&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:34.200
range. The half-life of plutonium-239 is&nbsp;
24400 years so it you don't get rid of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:43.200
on human time scales anyway. So yes, no one&nbsp;
will ever be able to inhabit those places.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:48.360
The state that Emu field and Monte Bello were&nbsp;
left in by the British was not much better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:53.760
and today there are still signs up warning&nbsp;
tourists not to stay longer than an hour.

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:58.380
Australia fronted many of the running&nbsp;
costs and gave political backing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:02.640
military personnel but was never in&nbsp;
charge or privy to the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:07.200
gained. The media was muzzled&nbsp;
by strict government censorship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:12.780
and the general public was kept in the dark&nbsp;
about the dangers posed by radioactive fallout.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:18.660
Military personnel involved in the tests were&nbsp;
exposed to dangerous radiation levels, and many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:23.640
have suffered severe health problems.&nbsp;
The indigenous communities living where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:27.660
the tests were carried out were not&nbsp;
consulted or informed of the dangers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:33.900
they faced. Many were forced to abandon their&nbsp;
ancestral lands and relocate to other areas.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:39.960
I think we all as Australians have an obligation&nbsp;
to make sure that this never happens again I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:45.300
it's a very very dark era in Australian&nbsp;
history and we need to guard against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:53.400
government secrecy and under the table agreements&nbsp;
between Australia and the so-called great powers.

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:58.500
Despite the passage of time, the true&nbsp;
extent of the damage caused by the tests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:04.080
remains unknown. The legacy of the nuclear&nbsp;
tests in Australia is a chilling reminder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:09.960
of the dangerous power physics has given us&nbsp;
and the lasting consequences of our actions.

